Restore the reserved length in ngx_http_script_run() and ngx_stream_script_run().

The fork's CVE-2026-42533 backport (44_nginx-1.27.1-CVE-2026-42533.patch)
rewrote both functions to allocate the script result plus `len` reserved bytes
and stop the codes at the end of the result, but it returns
value->len = e.pos - value->data, dropping the reserved bytes callers expect
in value->len. Upstream (nginx 1.30.4, release-1.30.4) returns
value->len = e.pos + len - value->data, as here.

Visible effect without this patch: an access_log path with variables loses
its last character, because the log modules reserve one byte for the
terminating NUL and write it at value->len - 1 (`access_log
/var/log/nginx/access-$host.log` writes to access-example.com.lo). verify.sh
checks this on the built controller image.

--- a/src/http/ngx_http_script.c
+++ b/src/http/ngx_http_script.c
@@ -670,7 +670,7 @@
         return NULL;
     }
 
-    value->len = e.pos - value->data;
+    value->len = e.pos + len - value->data;
 
     return e.pos;
 }
--- a/src/stream/ngx_stream_script.c
+++ b/src/stream/ngx_stream_script.c
@@ -546,7 +546,7 @@
         return NULL;
     }
 
-    value->len = e.pos - value->data;
+    value->len = e.pos + len - value->data;
 
     return e.pos;
 }
